    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "So, you're essentially saying \"inevitability\" is being used as a rhetorical tool—a \"call to action\"—to justify policies that currently lack a stable economic or physical foundation. \n\nIf we strip away the rhetorical framing, we're left with a situation where governments are mandating a transition that the current supply chains and energy densities cannot actually support. From a risk-management standpoint, wouldn't calling a policy-driven mandate \"inevitable\" be a way of delegitimizing the very real, data-driven concerns of investors who see this as a massive bubble? Are you saying that once a transition is deemed \"necessary,\" the actual economic or technical feasibility of the path becomes secondary?",
